WEBVTT

00:00:00.064 --> 00:00:01.907
This sentence can make you yawn.

00:00:01.917 --> 00:00:04.632
Seeing one, hearing one,
even reading about

00:00:04.642 --> 00:00:06.040
one can set it off.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.132
It may even jump species:

00:00:08.142 --> 00:00:10.982
in one study, twenty-one
of twenty-nine dogs

00:00:10.992 --> 00:00:12.819
yawned after a person did.

00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:15.468
The popular explanation is empathy:

00:00:15.505 --> 00:00:17.458
you catch yawns because you tune in

00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:18.444
to others.

00:00:18.454 --> 00:00:21.744
Then, in 2014, a Duke team tested

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:24.157
three hundred and
twenty-eight people with a

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:26.081
three-minute yawning video.

00:00:26.091 --> 00:00:29.664
They also measured empathy,
intelligence, sleepiness and

00:00:29.674 --> 00:00:30.784
time of day.

00:00:30.794 --> 00:00:33.101
Two hundred and twenty-two yawned at least

00:00:33.111 --> 00:00:33.867
once.

00:00:33.877 --> 00:00:35.747
But in this study, once age was

00:00:35.757 --> 00:00:38.392
allowed for, empathy made no difference.

00:00:38.402 --> 00:00:40.854
Age explained only eight percent.

00:00:40.864 --> 00:00:43.254
The rest is a mystery, and scientists

00:00:43.264 --> 00:00:44.977
still debate empathy.

00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:46.702
Feeling a yawn yet?
